diagnosis_text:
A thorough inspection of every component in the Office → Internet Lab path (192.168.3.x → 128.168.0.x) reveals a fully functioning network. Multiple office hosts (office_pc at .3.2, enquiry at .3.4, exam_cell at .3.3, tpo at .3.5) were tested against all four Internet Lab machines (pc2–pc5, .0.2–.0.5); every ping returned 0% packet loss with sub-4 ms RTTs. The forwarding plane on both router1 (the office gateway, eth0 = 192.168.3.1) and router2 (the Internet Lab gateway, eth1 = 128.168.0.1) contains correct, FIB-installed routes: router1 has a RIP-learned route to 128.168.0.0/16 via router2 (20.20.0.2, metric 2), and router2 has a RIP-learned route back to 192.168.3.0/24 via router1 (20.20.0.1, metric 2). RIP v2 is converged on all three routers with live neighbour timers (~3 minutes), correct network statements, and no route filters or redistributions. iptables on router1 is in full-ACCEPT policy with no rules. All end-host default gateways match the routers' actual interface addresses. The symptom described in helpdesk ticket #4618 — Office hosts unable to reach Internet Lab — does not correspond to any present or detectable fault. The network is healthy; no corrective action is required.
=== END ===